---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import SectionHead from '../../components/SectionHead.astro';
import Panel from '../../components/Panel.astro';
import ev from '../../data/security-evidence.json';

// Every number on this page comes from src/data/security-evidence.json (a
// recorded, sanitized run against the real edge) or from a dated test named
// next to it. Nothing here is illustrative.
const reqs = ev.waf.requests;
const SHORT: Record<string, string> = {
  baseline: 'normal page',
  env: '/.env',
  gitconfig: '/.git/config',
  sqli: 'SQL injection',
  xss: 'XSS',
  traversal: 'path traversal',
  log4shell: 'Log4Shell',
  cmdi: 'command injection',
  wordpress: 'wp-login probe',
  scanner_ua: 'scanner user agent',
};
const payloads = reqs.filter((r) => r.id !== 'baseline' && r.id !== 'wordpress');
const blockedPayloads = payloads.filter((r) => r.inband.verdict === 'blocked').length;
const ban = ev.ban_enforcement.reps;
const banMin = Math.min(...ban.map((r) => r.ban_to_drop_s));
const banMax = Math.max(...ban.map((r) => r.ban_to_drop_s));
const fw = ev.host_firewall.reps;
const fwClosed = fw.every(
  (r) => Object.values(r.nonadmin_guest_to_hypervisor).every((v) => v === 'closed'),
);
const red = ev.log_redaction.reps;
const sec = ev.secrets;
const kill = ev.egress_kill_test;
const s1 = (n: number) => n.toFixed(1);
// One date format on the page: MM-DD, the way every other date here reads.
const day = ev.captured.slice(5, 10);
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Homelab security"
  path="/projects/security/"
  description="The security stack in front of and inside my homelab, tested by attacking it: layered edge defenses, per-host firewalls, a SIEM and a scanned supply chain, plus four weaknesses my own testing caught and fixed."
  lede="The controls in front of and inside my homelab, tested by attacking them from outside. That testing caught four controls that were not doing their job; all four are fixed and re-tested. Every number on this page comes from a recorded, dated run."
  tags={['CrowdSec', 'OWASP CRS', 'Wazuh', 'nftables', 'Trivy', 'gitleaks']}
  kicker="Security · tested, not assumed"
  headline="Every control, attacked"
  accent="attacked"
  slug="security"
  stats={[
    { n: '4', l: 'weaknesses caught by my own testing, all fixed' },
    { n: `${blockedPayloads} / ${payloads.length}`, l: 'attack payloads blocked by the WAF, 403' },
    { n: `${s1(banMin)}-${s1(banMax)} s`, l: 'from ban decision to firewall drop' },
    { n: `${sec.findings_in_those_commits}`, l: `secrets in ${sec.commits_by_ethan_or_claude} of my commits` },
  ]}
  demonstrates="WAF tuning (OWASP CRS, detect then enforce), intrusion prevention with CrowdSec, host firewalls in nftables for IPv4 and IPv6, SIEM rules written and tested with planted events (Wazuh), CIS hardening, container vulnerability management (Trivy), secrets scanning (gitleaks), and controls mapped to CIS v8 and MITRE ATT&CK."
>
  <SectionHead label="The edge" title="Ten requests, one edge" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> four layers stand between the internet and
    any app, and each was tested from outside.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything public sits behind one reverse proxy VM. A request has to clear
    four layers before it reaches an app: a geo filter in nftables covering
    TCP 443, UDP 443 (HTTP/3) and TCP 80; a CrowdSec ban list enforced in both
    the firewall and the proxy; a WAF running CrowdSec virtual patches and the
    OWASP Core Rule Set in blocking mode; and TLS termination with credentials
    redacted before anything is written to a log.
  </p>
  <p>
    I sent ten requests through the edge's WAF engine, using the same API the
    proxy calls, six times each. Every attack payload was blocked with a 403
    before it could reach an app, with the same result in all six runs. The
    table has every request, verdict, score and rule.
    <a href="/">Watch one go through the layers on the home page</a>.
  </p>

  <Panel label="edge · recorded run" status={`captured ${day}`}>
    <table class="ev-table">
      <caption>All ten requests, as recorded. Rule IDs are OWASP CRS unless named.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Request</th><th scope="col">WAF</th><th scope="col">CRS score</th><th scope="col">Rules that fired</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          reqs.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{SHORT[r.id]}</th>
              <td class:list={[r.inband.verdict === 'blocked' && 'is-blocked']}>
                {r.inband.verdict === 'blocked' ? `blocked, ${r.inband.http}` : `allowed, ${r.inband.http}`}
              </td>
              <td>{r.crs.anomaly_score}</td>
              <td class="ev-rules">
                {r.inband.rules.map((x) => (x.id.startsWith('crowdsecurity/') ? x.name : x.id)).join(', ') || 'none'}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="sample-note">
      Method: {ev.waf.method} Caddy {ev.versions.caddy}, CrowdSec {ev.versions.crowdsec},
      {ev.versions.appsec_rules_loaded} WAF rules loaded.
    </p>
  </Panel>

  <p>
    The one request that is allowed asks for a WordPress login page. By itself
    that is not an attack payload; scanning for it is, and CrowdSec's
    wordpress-scan scenario catches that pattern from the proxy logs. Four
    blocked requests from one source earn a 24-hour ban, and the firewall
    starts dropping that source {s1(banMin)} to {s1(banMax)} seconds after the
    decision (three measured runs).
  </p>
  <p>
    How the WAF got to blocking: the Core Rule Set ran in detection-only mode
    first, scoring real traffic without blocking it. It flagged none of about
    5,300 real requests, so on {ev.waf.tuning.switched_in_band.slice(5, 10)} it
    moved to blocking. Normal requests to every public site afterwards: none
    blocked. Detect, tune, then enforce is the order I would want at work too.
  </p>

  <SectionHead label="Found by attacking it" title="Four weaknesses my own testing caught" />
  <p>
    Each of these looked fine in the configuration and broke the first time I
    attacked it from the outside. All four are fixed and re-tested. For each:
    what was wrong, how it showed up, the fix, and the re-test.
  </p>

  <div class="finds">
    <article class="find" data-card>
      <p class="label">fail2ban · edge</p>
      <h3>The ban that never was</h3>
      <p class="find-tldr">fail2ban logged bans and enforced none of them.</p>
      <dl>
        <dt>Wrong</dt>
        <dd>The edge VM is nftables-only. fail2ban's default action calls iptables, exited with code 127, and logged "Ban" anyway. No ban on that VM had ever been enforced.</dd>
        <dt>Found</dt>
        <dd>A family member got "banned" while streaming. They were never actually blocked, and neither was anyone else.</dd>
        <dt>Fix</dt>
        <dd>nftables action for TCP and UDP; the jail counts only 401 and 403, not the 404s a media client produces after an upgrade.</dd>
        <dt>Re-test</dt>
        <dd>A phone on cellular sent five bad logins: banned in 10 s, then 0 requests reached the proxy. (10-30, one run.)</dd>
      </dl>
    </article>

    <article class="find" data-card>
      <p class="label">host firewall · every guest</p>
      <h3>IPv6 walked around the admin allowlist</h3>
      <p class="find-tldr">The IPv4 allowlist was right. IPv6 skipped it.</p>
      <dl>
        <dt>Wrong</dt>
        <dd>Admin ports were pinned to three hosts over IPv4. The generator put the same ports in the IPv6 LAN rule, so any phone or TV could reach SSH and the hypervisor UI over link-local IPv6.</dd>
        <dt>Found</dt>
        <dd>A firewall review that tested IPv6, not only IPv4.</dd>
        <dt>Fix</dt>
        <dd>IPv6 gets service ports only. Rolled out host by host, each with a 10-minute auto-revert armed.</dd>
        <dt>Re-test</dt>
        <dd>
          From a non-admin container, hypervisor SSH and web UI over IPv4 and
          IPv6: {fwClosed ? 'closed' : 'NOT closed'} in {fw.length} of {fw.length} runs. Controls (a service port it needs, admin SSH) stayed open. ({day})
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </article>

    <article class="find" data-card>
      <p class="label">reverse proxy · logs</p>
      <h3>The proxy was logging the keys</h3>
      <p class="find-tldr">Credentials sat in world-readable logs. Now they are hashed or dropped before anything is written.</p>
      <dl>
        <dt>Wrong</dt>
        <dd>Media clients send API keys in query strings and headers. The access log kept them verbatim in world-readable files: 1,136 media-server keys, 141 auth tokens and 4,468 admin API keys.</dd>
        <dt>Found</dt>
        <dd>A secrets audit that read the logs, not just the configs.</dd>
        <dt>Fix</dt>
        <dd>Redact at the logger (query values hashed, credential headers dropped), tighten permissions, and mask 25,888 old values in place without breaking the three tools that parse those logs.</dd>
        <dt>Re-test</dt>
        <dd>
          {red.length} requests from outside the network with fake credentials:
          each logged once, {red.reduce((a, r) => a + r.fake_values_found, 0)} fake values in any log, key stored as an {red[0].apikey_as_logged.split(', ')[1].replace(' chars', '-character')} hash. ({day})
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </article>

    <article class="find" data-card>
      <p class="label">geo filter · edge</p>
      <h3>HTTP/3 walked past the geo filter</h3>
      <p class="find-tldr">The country filter covered TCP only. HTTP/3 runs over UDP.</p>
      <dl>
        <dt>Wrong</dt>
        <dd>The rule matched TCP 443. The proxy also speaks HTTP/3 over UDP 443, so a client outside the allowed countries could still connect.</dd>
        <dt>Found</dt>
        <dd>Reading which ports the proxy listens on, then testing each one.</dd>
        <dt>Fix</dt>
        <dd>Filter TCP and UDP 443 plus TCP 80. Closing port 80 meant moving certificates to the DNS-01 challenge first.</dd>
        <dt>Re-test</dt>
        <dd>A phone on cellular, cut out of the allow list: TCP 443, UDP 443 and TCP 80 all dropped at the firewall. (10-30, one run.)</dd>
      </dl>
    </article>
  </div>


  <SectionHead label="Inside the perimeter" title="Assume the edge fails" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> if something gets past the edge, every host
    still denies by default, is hardened, and reports to a SIEM.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every guest runs its own default-deny nftables table, loaded by its own
    service, so the hypervisor is not the only thing between a service and the
    network. Admin ports are reachable from three hosts. It ran in audit mode
    for 46 hours before enforcing, and the enforcing rollout fired zero
    auto-reverts.
  </p>
  <p>
    All eight hosts are hardened against the CIS benchmarks in two tiers: SSH
    key-only for root, account lockout, audit rules, password length over
    composition rules (NIST SP 800-63B). Wazuh's configuration assessment
    scored every host 40 to 43 before and 51 to 55 after.
  </p>
  <p>
    Wazuh collects from all eight hosts. On 10-31 I planted a file in the
    systemd unit directory of every one of them; all eight raised an alert
    within 2 seconds. On the hypervisor I planted more on 10-30: touching sudoers raised a level 10 alert, running a binary from
    /tmp a level 12, and an EICAR test file a level 12 VirusTotal hit (66 of 68
    engines). The tuning goes the other way too: a monitoring agent's daily
    updater runs a script from /tmp, so one rule matches only that exact
    command shape. The same shape with any other script name still fires
    level 12.
  </p>

  <SectionHead label="Supply chain" title="What runs here, and what I publish" />
  <p>
    Trivy scans every container image daily. Most critical CVEs in public
    images need the upstream project to rebuild, so a weekly reminder became
    noise. The alert is now keyed to the exact image and CVE pairs: fewer CVEs
    stay quiet, a new one alerts at once. Updates and automatic rollbacks are on
    the <a href="/projects/homelab/">homelab page</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    I scanned every commit of my four public repositories with gitleaks
    {sec.tool.replace('gitleaks ', '')}: {sec.commits_scanned.toLocaleString('en-US')}
    commits, {sec.commits_by_ethan_or_claude} of them mine. Findings in my commits:
    {sec.findings_in_those_commits}. The other {sec.findings_in_upstream_history} findings
    are test fixtures and saved recipe pages in the forked projects' own history.
    This site has its own gate: a build fails if a private word or an inline
    script reaches the output.
  </p>

  <SectionHead label="Mapped" title="Control by control" />
  <div class="map-table-wrap">
    <table class="ev-table map-table">
      <caption>CIS Controls v8 safeguards and the ATT&CK techniques each control is there for. "Tested" means a recorded run with a date, not a configuration review.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Control</th><th scope="col">CIS v8</th><th scope="col">ATT&CK</th><th scope="col">Tested</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Geo filter, TCP + UDP</th><td>4.4</td><td>T1595</td><td>10-30, phone on cellular</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">CrowdSec + bouncers</th><td>13.3, 13.8</td><td>T1595.002, T1190</td><td>{day}, ban timing x3</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">WAF: virtual patches + OWASP CRS</th><td>13.10</td><td>T1190</td><td>{day}, 10 requests x6</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">fail2ban</th><td>13.8</td><td>T1110</td><td>10-30, phone on cellular</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Per-guest firewall, IPv4 + IPv6</th><td>4.4, 13.4</td><td>T1021.004</td><td>{day}, x3</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">VPN egress, default drop</th><td>13.4</td><td>none</td><td>{kill.captured.slice(5, 10)}, 6 tunnel kills</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">CIS hardening</th><td>4.1</td><td>T1110, T1548.003</td><td>10-22 to 10-25, Wazuh SCA</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Wazuh SIEM</th><td>8.2, 8.9, 13.1</td><td>T1548.003, T1105</td><td>10-30, planted events</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Log redaction + permissions</th><td>3.3</td><td>T1552.001</td><td>{day}, x3 from outside</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Trivy + weekly updater</th><td>7.4, 7.5</td><td>T1190</td><td>daily scans; rollback forced 10-22</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Secrets scan + site gate</th><td>16.12</td><td>T1552.001</td><td>{sec.captured.slice(5, 10)}, every commit</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Backups, restore drills</th><td>11.5</td><td>T1485, T1486</td><td>10-22 and 10-30, timed restores</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>


</ProjectLayout>
